#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c30217a-484c-5c31-a2bf-413f04e0edea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baf9d002-bcf5-5f90-b49e-8c3c5f9e1bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353644} {:answer/id #uuid "fcdf32a2-dc26-590f-90a0-354b79c03aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353645} {:answer/id #uuid "da4d4bcd-ead5-5014-84ce-51e005d525ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353646} {:answer/id #uuid "46b9fb42-63e3-5909-980f-054ca2cace6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353647} {:answer/id #uuid "4ace2a20-b315-556b-ac18-73eb75679ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcdf32a2-dc26-590f-90a0-354b79c03aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353645} :answer-field/id #uuid "0afe9224-2b41-5c7c-8ff1-4514c21b5822" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b72bbf8-5b68-57d9-9c85-19249589cfdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-18581" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(0,0,1)$, $M(2,1,1)$, $N(3,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(2i+j+k)-k \\\\  & =2i+j \\\\  & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(3i+j+k)-k \\\\  & =3i+j \\\\  & =⟨3,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 3)j+(2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c30217a-484c-5c31-a2bf-413f04e0edea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa03f5a-1a84-50d0-be05-5104bab8e9d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102526} {:answer/id #uuid "cd62ca8e-c1d8-549b-a714-ac7cbba72e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102527} {:answer/id #uuid "e929a503-cbf3-5d4d-90a4-e0990316360e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102528} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b082e7-bfc6-519b-94e7-3919cf44ee61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102529} {:answer/id #uuid "706bf228-8405-5a78-9089-63fe2c848227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa03f5a-1a84-50d0-be05-5104bab8e9d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102526} :answer-field/id #uuid "03d0c442-32b8-5c7e-ae91-cffd2c10a5c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7799a52e-21d3-584f-93c3-f00bb69321c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-20026" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,PQR$, if $P(4,7,3)$, $Q(5,6,2)$, $R(3,8,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(5i+6j+2k)-(4i+7j+3k) \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(3i+8j+3k)-(4i+7j+3k) \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))i-(1\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-1))j+(1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =i+j \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9331987e-8676-5bdb-b4ce-2129b1b09a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Altitude of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a0b034a-5030-571f-b292-0332a3dcc303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{299}{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a0b034a-5030-571f-b292-0332a3dcc303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{299}{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353651} :answer-field/id #uuid "905dbcbf-aeb5-5444-b3ea-56106a27b5f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f3099d2-cb48-59c2-9883-9eee50d05767" :question/math-academy-id "q-316395" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $UT$ of the $△\\,UVW$, if $U(-2,2,0)$, $V(1,-1,2)$, $W(3,0,-1).$\n\n$UT=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $UT$ can be related to the area of $△\\,UVW$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $UT,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW} & =w-v \\\\  & =(3i-k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+j-3k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =u-v \\\\  & =(-2i+2j)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 3)i-(2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+(2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =7i+13j+9k \\\\  & =⟨7,13,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{13}^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{299}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,UVW$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $VW$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}VW & =|\\overset{\\to }{VW}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{299} & =UT\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ UT & =\\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4186}}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c30217a-484c-5c31-a2bf-413f04e0edea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed749cfd-811e-5515-8902-cc1a267e28ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353654} {:answer/id #uuid "e8dba0a6-b840-5234-bdc6-5a7245047d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353655} {:answer/id #uuid "17b1ce24-00f4-57e8-9f9f-45fd355f05a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353656} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5c6f2f-def8-51e9-b0c8-74894d481308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353657} {:answer/id #uuid "f11196de-2f0d-5e75-960b-db8d744eca7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f11196de-2f0d-5e75-960b-db8d744eca7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353658} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc8496f-8524-5c40-b815-8aaa6339729e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1faa6cb6-00d2-51ca-a935-3b7f2f66f1a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71111" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,CDE$, if $C(1,-2,3)$, $D(1,-3,3)$, $E(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =d-c \\\\  & =(i-3j+3k)-(i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =-j \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =e-c \\\\  & =(i+j+k)-(i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)i-(0\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)j+(0\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =2i \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,CDE$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9331987e-8676-5bdb-b4ce-2129b1b09a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Altitude of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4216c0e4-2015-5109-a1d4-88cba8f205d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353661} {:answer/id #uuid "457292af-b8c0-59b2-a1ea-171c65e5b64e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353662} {:answer/id #uuid "0e860a9a-26cc-5025-9c4c-447aa44523cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353663} {:answer/id #uuid "56f6ef1c-cf3d-596c-a618-241147d54c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353664} {:answer/id #uuid "1110598f-1824-528f-ab83-5b354f4c024a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1110598f-1824-528f-ab83-5b354f4c024a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353665} :answer-field/id #uuid "664b8096-0325-553e-a503-7f0c65ae15d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df2dfeec-fe0c-5997-9a8d-575665c546df" :question/math-academy-id "q-71113" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $MH$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(3,3,2)$, $M(1,1,2)$, $N(2,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(i+j+2k)-(3i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(2i+2j+k)-(3i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
